When Your Vendor Becomes Your Attack Surface

InfinityGrid Lesson: IG-2026-024
Case Study: Adobe / Mr. Raccoon allegation
Category: Third-Party Risk / Identity / Least Privilege
Important: The underlying Adobe compromise remains unconfirmed. This lesson analyzes the reported attack pattern and independently observed BPO risk.

The Security Problem

Organizations outsource:

Customer support
Helpdesks
Call centers
Technical support
Billing
Identity verification
IT operations

Those providers frequently receive legitimate access to corporate systems.

The security boundary therefore becomes:

YOUR COMPANY
     +
YOUR VENDOR
     +
THEIR EMPLOYEES
     +
THEIR ENDPOINTS
     +
THEIR IDENTITIES
     =
YOUR EFFECTIVE ATTACK SURFACE
The Trusted-Relationship Attack

A traditional attacker might attempt:

Internet
↓
Firewall
↓
Exploit
↓
Internal Network

A trusted-provider attack can instead become:

Vendor Employee
      ↓
Phishing
      ↓
Credential / Session Theft
      ↓
Legitimate Authentication
      ↓
Customer Environment

The attacker doesn't necessarily break through your front door.

They enter through somebody who already has a key.

Least Privilege

Support personnel should receive access to:

what they need

not:

everything the support platform contains.

Preferred model:

Support Agent
     ↓
Assigned Customers
     ↓
Assigned Cases
     ↓
Required Data

Dangerous model:

Support Agent
     ↓
Global Search
     ↓
Historical Dataset
     ↓
Bulk Export
Vendor Identity Controls

Third-party accounts should be treated as high-risk identities.

Recommended controls include:

Phishing-resistant MFA
FIDO2 / passkeys
Dedicated vendor identities
Conditional Access
Device compliance
Managed endpoints
Restricted locations
Just-in-time privilege
Privileged Identity Management
Session monitoring
Automatic account expiration
Regular access certification
Bulk Data Protection

A legitimate account should not automatically mean unlimited data access.

Monitor for:

Massive ticket searches
Historical-data enumeration
Abnormal API activity
Bulk exports
Rapid downloads
Repeated queries
Unusual working hours
New device fingerprints
New geographic locations
Support Tickets Are Sensitive

Support tickets can contain:

Names
Email addresses
Screenshots
Log files
Configuration details
Account identifiers
Internal URLs
Product versions
Error messages
Infrastructure names
Troubleshooting history

Treat support systems as sensitive production repositories.

Vulnerability Research Requires Separate Protection

Private vulnerability information should not casually share the same trust boundaries as ordinary customer-support data.

Ideal separation:

Customer Support
      │
      X
      │
Private Vulnerability Research

Security research repositories should receive:

Separate authorization
Strong authentication
Minimal membership
Export restrictions
Extensive auditing
Data-loss prevention
Continuous access review
Defender Checklist

Inventory every external organization with authenticated access.

Inventory every third-party identity.

Require phishing-resistant MFA where possible.

Require managed/compliant endpoints.

Remove global data access from ordinary support roles.

Alert on abnormal bulk exports.

Apply automatic expiration to contractor accounts.

Review third-party privileges regularly.

Separate vulnerability intelligence from support systems.

Test what happens when a vendor identity is compromised.

Include BPO providers in tabletop exercises.

Continuously monitor trusted relationships.

The Lesson

Third-party risk is not merely:

"Does our vendor have SOC 2?"

The operational question is:

"If one of their employees is compromised tomorrow, what can the attacker reach inside our environment?"

That is the attack path defenders need to model.

